            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Reads a text file of numbers - one row per line, values separated by commas, tabs or spaces - into a 2-D array. CSV files from a spreadsheet, a data logger or another program; files written by np.savetxt.\n\nA cell that is empty or is not a number becomes nan, rather than stopping the whole load: np.isnan finds those, np.nan_to_num fills them, and the nan- nodes skip them. (It reads with numpy's genfromtxt, which is the forgiving reader.)\n\nLOADING:\nClick load and choose a file; it is read and sent at once. send sends the same array again without re-reading. A path in the path option, or sent as the message \"path\n<file>\", is read straight away - from a patch, with no dialog - and is read again\nwhen the patch opens.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.loadtxt <path>\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nload (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file, then reads and sends it.\n\nsend (button, also an inlet):\nSends the array again.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: the file to read. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from, which is why the demo's path starts with dpg_system/help.\n\ndelimiter:\nAn option, under the node: comma (the default), tab, or any space (any run of spaces and tabs).\n\nskip rows:\nAn option, under the node: how many lines to skip at the top - 1 for a header line of column names.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray:\nThe table: one row per line. A single column, or a single line, comes out as a 1-D list.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nAt load the message sets the path to a small table in the help folder: a header line (speed,height), then six rows. skip rows is 1, so the header is left out. The fourth row has no height, which comes out as nan.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.savetxt writes text tables. np.load reads numpy's own files; text_file reads any text line by line.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Reads a text file of numbers - one row per line, values separated by commas, tabs or spaces - into a 2-D array. CSV files from a spreadsheet, a data logger or another program; files written by np.savetxt.\n\nA cell that is empty or is not a number becomes nan, rather than stopping the whole load: np.isnan finds those, np.nan_to_num fills them, and the nan- nodes skip them. (It reads with numpy's genfromtxt, which is the forgiving reader.)\n\nLOADING:\nClick load and choose a file; it is read and sent at once. send sends the same array again without re-reading. A path in the path option, or sent as the message \"path\n<file>\", is read straight away - from a patch, with no dialog - and is read again\nwhen the patch opens.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.loadtxt <path>\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nload (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file, then reads and sends it.\n\nsend (button, also an inlet):\nSends the array again.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: the file to read. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from, which is why the demo's path starts with dpg_system/help.\n\ndelimiter:\nAn option, under the node: comma (the default), tab, or any space (any run of spaces and tabs).\n\nskip rows:\nAn option, under the node: how many lines to skip at the top - 1 for a header line of column names.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray:\nThe table: one row per line. A single column, or a single line, comes out as a 1-D list.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nAt load the message sets the path to a small table in the help folder: a header line (speed,height), then six rows. skip rows is 1, so the header is left out. The fourth row has no height, which comes out as nan.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.savetxt writes text tables. np.load reads numpy's own files; text_file reads any text line by line."
        },
